**Goal:** Math++ is an interpreted maths language written only in Rust and built with Cargo. A `.mpp` file reads like an academic paper: light prose markup with maths in it, and nothing that looks like code. The interpreter reads the file, keeps track of variables and functions, works out results (algebra, derivatives, integrals, series, limits) and draws 2D and 3D graphs. An IDE shows the typeset result live as you type and lets you jump between files you import.

**Repo (Ben, 2026-11-20):** `https://github.com/Benjamin-Elmore/MathPlusPlus` (private, empty when cloned), cloned to `C:\Elmore\MathPP`. This queue lives in it at `work/RUN-STATE.mpp`.

**.mpp only (Ben, 2026-11-20):** no Markdown files anywhere in the repo. The README, grammar, guide, plan, reviews and examples are all `.mpp` (`README.mpp`, `docs/grammar.mpp`, `work/PLAN.mpp`, `work/reviews/M-VAL.mpp`…). The `.mpp` prose markup (headings, emphasis, lists, tables, links) is Math++'s own syntax with its own parser, with no Markdown crate.

**File vs equation view (Ben, 2026-11-20):** what the file contains and what you see are different. The file holds plain text (`\frac{a}{b}`, `alpha`, `Figure: …`), and the preview shows typeset maths, filled-in results and drawn graphs. **The IDE shows only the equation view** (Ben, 2026-11-20: "Don't automatically show raw text. Just show the equation view"). You type straight into the typeset page, which looks like an academic paper. The raw text is **never shown automatically**: not on start-up, not on open, not on errors, not in pop-ups. The only way to see it is a deliberate menu action (View → Show file text), which is off by default and turns itself off again when the app restarts.

**Run cycle (same lean cycle as the site):**
1. **Plan (Opus, high):** M1 settles the grammar, the crate layout and the order, then writes `work/PLAN.mpp` (short). Any change from the defaults below is noted there.
2. **Build:** items not yet done run in parallel following **"Parallel plan"** below (Ben, 2026-11-20). The list that follows is the dependency order inside a lane and the order phases are integrated and PR'd: M0 → M11, N1 → N5, C1 → C4, C7, C8, M12 → M13, C5, M14, C10, C9, C6, M15, N16, N12, N6 → N8, N14, N9, N13, N10, N11, N15 (N items added by Ben while the queue was running, 2026-11-20). Every N item also adds its part to `docs/guide.mpp` and at least one example in `examples/` with its expected output. Work on **branches**, one per phase (see "Branches + PRs" below). Commit per item, describing the change in the message (no M numbers, no Claude trailers or "Generated with" lines, in commits or PRs). **Push after every commit** (Ben, 2026-11-20: "so I can track it outside of the terminal"): every commit on any branch (phase branches and `wip/*` lane branches) is pushed to GitHub straight away with the push command below, using `git push -u origin <branch>` the first time. This includes run-state updates. Pushing a branch is not opening a PR: PRs still open only when a phase is green. If a push fails (network, auth), note it, carry on, and push again after the next commit. The `Builder` column gives the model for each item. Each item ends green on `cargo build`, `cargo test`, `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` and `cargo fmt --check`, checked by a Haiku checkpoint. When a phase is green, push its branch and open its PR with `gh pr create`.
3. **Validate:** M16 runs the checks, then the M17 subagent panel and its fix loop (on its own branch and PR). Fixers start on Sonnet and move to Opus from the 2nd attempt, or straight away for any Blocker.
Run end to end without prompting Ben, and park anything that needs him under Needs Ben. Push branches and open PRs, but **never merge**: Ben merges. Push with `git -c credential.helper= -c "credential.helper=!gh auth git-credential" push` (plain HTTPS has no credentials on this PC).

**Branches + PRs:** the PRs are stacked, so each phase branches from the one before it and its PR targets that branch. When Ben merges them in order, GitHub retargets each one to `main`. Each PR description is short: what it adds, how to try it, and its test results.
