### Parallel plan (Ben, 2026-11-20: "work on multiple sections of the codebase at the same time")
Added while the queue was running. It applies to every item not yet done. Done items and the stacked-PR layout above stay as they are.

**How it works**
- **Lanes:** each lane is one builder subagent in its own git worktree (`.claude/worktrees/…`) on a `wip/<lane>` branch, as `wip/core` and `wip/render` already are. A lane owns its crate or module, and **only that lane edits it** while the lane is active, so lanes don't collide.
- **Waves:** a wave starts its lanes together. A lane inside a wave starts as soon as its own "needs" are integrated; it doesn't wait for the whole previous wave. Run **at most 4 builder lanes at once** (Rust builds are heavy on CPU and disk), and give each worktree its own `target/` folder.
- **Integrator (the main session, Sonnet):** merges each finished lane into its phase branch **in phase order** (`feat/engine` → `feat/calculus` → `feat/domains` → `feat/calc-tools` → `feat/render-plot` → `feat/ide-cli` → `feat/docs-export` → `feat/ide-tools`). It re-runs the four cargo checks on the phase branch (Haiku checkpoint), then pushes and opens that phase's PR once all of its items are in and green. If an earlier phase branch changes later, merge it forward into the later ones. Never merge to `main`.
- **Shared files are integrator-only:** the workspace `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`, `work/RUN-STATE.mpp` (State column), `docs/grammar.mpp` and `docs/guide.mpp`. A lane that needs a new crate dependency or a grammar or guide change writes it in `work/lanes/<item>.mpp`, and the integrator applies it during the merge. (The N1/N6 grammar addenda go this way.)
- **Interfaces first:** a lane that needs something not yet built (for example the IDE needing the layout engine) codes against the agreed trait or type from `work/PLAN.mpp` and uses a stub until the real one lands. Stubs are deleted when the real one is integrated.
- Each lane still ends each item green on build/test/clippy/fmt in its worktree, commits per item (no item numbers, no trailers), and uses the Builder model from the item row.
- **Every lane pushes its `wip/<lane>` branch to GitHub after each commit** (Ben, 2026-11-20), so Ben can follow each lane on GitHub. The integrator does the same for the phase branches.

